\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\title{Typed harness adaptation and total lifecycle accounting}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{harness}
\tag{state}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{A harness adaptation claim must keep the executable solver, the proposer
that edits its harness, the retained harness state \ref{ftip-00C8}, and the
evaluator \ref{ftip-001T} as
different objects. A change in one object is not evidence that the others
changed. The [stateful-search convention](ftip-00OW) already records durable
research state and replay; the comparison therefore fixes an adaptation and
accounting boundary for interpreting evidence.}

\p{\strong{Typed roles and declared endowment.} Let #{M} be a fixed solver
artifact, #{\theta} the parameters of a proposer, #{h_t\in\mathcal H} the
versioned harness state after inner round #{t}, and #{q\in\mathcal Q} a task.
The proposer is a revision policy}
##{
\pi_\theta:\mathcal Q\times\mathcal H\times\mathcal R
\longrightarrow \mathsf{Dist}(\mathcal Y),
}
\p{where #{\mathcal R} is a typed execution-report space and #{\mathcal Y}
contains candidate edits. Applying an edit to a parent state produces a
candidate #{\widetilde h_t}; a validation and selection rule either commits
#{h_t=\widetilde h_t} or retains #{h_t=h_{t-1}}. The solver #{M} and final
proposer parameters #{\theta_*} are fixed during this inner loop.}

\p{For the declared outer training, define}
##{
O_{\rm outer}=(\theta_0,\ldots,\theta_K,D_{\rm train},\xi_{<K},
L_{\rm outer},C_{\rm outer}),
}
\p{The final proposer is #{\theta_*:=\theta_K}, and #{L_{\rm outer}} records
prompts, generated and failed revisions, and teacher filtering. The declared
endowment for an inner-loop comparison is}
##{
E_0=(M,\theta_*,h_0,\kappa_0,\mathcal Q_0,\mathcal P_0,\rho_0,O_{\rm outer}),
}
\p{where #{\kappa_0} contains implementation and harness versions,
#{\mathcal Q_0} the task and split laws, #{\mathcal P_0} permissions and
available tools, and #{\rho_0} seed, API, tokenizer, and evaluator state.
Existing solver code, seed harnesses, accessible examples, and checker
interfaces are part of #{E_0}; if an arm constructs any of them, that
preparation is a charged outer operation recorded in #{C_{\rm outer}}. An
adaptation result is therefore conditional on the same declared endowment,
including its outer-training trace, event reports, and cost, even when the
retained state is later changed.}

\p{\strong{Outer training and inner adaptation.} Outer training updates only
the proposer, for example}
##{
\theta_{k+1}=U(\theta_k;D_{\rm train},\xi_k),
}
\p{using proposer or teacher data and execution feedback. Its outer-training
trace is included in #{E_0}; at test time the inner loop starts from the
declared seed #{h_0}. For a finite nonempty
candidate index set #{J_t}, it repeatedly records}
##{
R_t=\operatorname{report}(M,h_{t-1},q_{\rm fb,t},\xi_t),\qquad
\{y_{t,j}\}_{j\in J_t}\sim\pi_{\theta_*}^{\otimes J_t}(q,h_{t-1},R_t),\qquad
h_t=\operatorname{select}\bigl(h_{t-1},
\{\operatorname{apply}(h_{t-1},y_{t,j})\}_{j\in J_t};q_{\rm score,t}\bigr).
}
\p{An outer teacher, when used for supervised initialization, is a separate
role from the proposer and solver. Its prompts, generated revisions, failed
revisions, and filtering work remain in #{L_{\rm outer}}. Neither an outer
weight update nor an inner harness edit may be described as a model-weight
capability change.}

\p{\strong{Leakage controls.} A reported comparison declares pairwise-disjoint
sets #{Q_{\rm train}}, #{Q_{\rm fb}}, #{Q_{\rm score}}, #{Q_{\rm dev}}, and
#{Q_{\rm eval}} (or records the exact exception). Feedback and score questions
may select or reject a candidate during adaptation; development questions may
select a frozen proposer or protocol. Final evaluation questions are revealed
only after the adapted state and its configuration are frozen, and their
outcomes cannot revise that state in the same run. The task family, solver,
proposer, seed harness, checker, and split law are reported with every score.}

\p{\strong{Provenance and rollback.} Each committed state carries a record}
##{
V_t=(\operatorname{digest}(E_0,M,\theta_*,h_t,\kappa_t),
E_0,
L_t,\{y_i,R_i,d_i\}_{i<t},C_{\leq t}),
}
\p{where the digest also covers the declared endowment #{E_0}, including its
task and split laws, permissions, tools, and seed and evaluator state. #{L_t}
is the event prefix, #{d_i} records validation and commit
decisions, and #{C_{\leq t}} is the cumulative cost record. A rejected edit
leaves the parent state in place but remains in the event log. A proposed
rollback, as specified in \ref{ftip-00D7}, to #{V_j} restores the complete named snapshot and its configuration;
it cannot erase the proposal, execution, storage, or evaluator costs already
incurred. Rollback is an FTIP protocol proposal, not a result established by
the source papers.}

\p{\strong{Total accounting.} Refine the lifecycle vector
#{C(P)=(C_{\rm data},C_{\rm feedback},C_{\rm rollout},C_{\rm env},
C_{\rm update},C_{\rm storage},C_{\rm eval})} from
\ref{ftip-005H} by recording a role-indexed vector for}
##{
\mathbf C_{\rm total}
=\sum_{r\in\{\rm teacher,prop_{outer},prop_{inner},solver,tool,evaluator\}}
\mathbf C^{r}.
}
\p{Every component keeps its declared unit. The account includes initial
endowment preparation when it is arm-specific, proposer and teacher calls,
solver and tool executions, evaluator calls, context and artifact storage,
retries, invalid edits, rejected candidates, failed experiments, and any
development work. Cached and uncached tokens, wall time, FLOPs, bytes, and
calls are not silently converted into one scalar. A price or scalar utility
may be reported only after this typed vector and the split of each role are
available.}

\p{\strong{Finite typed example.} Fix #{M}, #{\theta_*}, and #{h_0} in the
endowment. In round one, the proposer emits an invalid edit #{y_1^a} and a
valid edit #{y_1^b}; the first execution and rejection are charged, while
selection commits #{h_1=\operatorname{apply}(h_0,y_1^b)}. In round two, noisy
feedback produces #{y_2}; an adversarial score check rejects it, so
#{h_2=h_1}. A later audit may restore #{V_1}, but the costs of #{y_1^a},
#{y_1^b}, #{y_2}, their executions, and the checks remain in
#{\mathbf C_{\rm total}}. Evaluating #{h_1} on fresh #{Q_{\rm eval}} tests the
frozen state; it does not prove that the proposer learned a general solver.}

\p{\strong{Source observations.} Harness Learning defines a harness as the
executable program that organizes solver calls, tools, and information flow,
trains a proposer in an outer loop, and uses execution feedback to revise a
fixed solver harness in an inner loop. Its test-time proposer and solver stay
fixed while the harness changes; the paper reports Reasoning Gym and
multi-hop question-answering experiments, including held-out and unseen-task
comparisons (\citet{Sections 3-4.3 and 5-6}{zhang2026harnesslearning}).}

\p{ReHarness reports a different intervention: under a strictly online stream,
forward read and write are coupled with backward revision of reflections using
the current trajectory. Its three continual-learning benchmarks and three
backbones report source-grounded revisions and failures under distribution
shift, but the result remains a benchmarked online protocol
(\citet{Sections 3-5}{xiao2026reharness}). Stateless Language Agents likewise
place durable research state in the harness and reconstruct role-specific
contexts for stateless calls; their protected evaluation and token records
motivate the provenance fields, while their mostly single-run settings and
omitted experiment/evaluation compute limit the cost conclusion
(\citet{Sections 3-5, Appendix B.1, and limitations}{zhang2026statelessagents}).}

\p{\strong{FTIP deduction and boundary.} These observations support separating
outer training, inner adaptation, state provenance, feedback, and lifecycle
costs. The conceptual-discovery boundary in \ref{ftip-00MH} remains separate:
these results do not establish a universal self-improvement law, transfer to
frontier reasoning, or a monotone relation between more revisions and
capability. The source results are finite empirical observations; the typed
state record, leakage controls, complete cost decomposition, and rollback
test are FTIP protocol proposals. The checked Forest, formal-math,
TauCetiReview, and TauCetiWorker sources contain no declaration corresponding
to these empirical harness mechanisms or their total-cost record; existing
Forest state, replay, version, and lifecycle conventions are reused without
adding a formalization claim.}
